-<section class="card" id="indication"><div class="eyebrow">01 / Define the indication before counting people</div><h2>A condition, a treatment pathway, an eligible subset</h2><div class="grid"><div><p>For this study, we rely on publicly available information and carefully documented clinical and economic assumptions. Our main reference is a pharmacoeconomic review published by <strong>Canada’s Drug Agency (CDA-AMC), formerly CADTH</strong>. We focus on <strong>hidradenitis suppurativa (HS)</strong>, a chronic inflammatory skin condition, and <strong>secukinumab</strong>, marketed as <strong>Cosentyx</strong>. <a data-source="review" data-page="9">Review, pp. 9–12, 143–148</a>.</p><p>We illustrate how an economist could construct a BC budget impact analysis; we do not possess actual provincial patient-level records. The reviewed indication is adults with moderate-to-severe HS who responded inadequately to conventional systemic therapy. A diagnosis alone does not establish biologic eligibility or public reimbursement.</p></div><div id="care-art" class="panel"></div></div>
-<h3>Clinical severity: a physician’s assessment</h3><div id="severity-clinician" class="illustration-scroll"></div><p>Hurley stage and inadequate treatment response usually cannot be established from diagnostic codes alone. Clinical documentation refines the estimated cohort. Current BC initial targeted-treatment criteria require Hurley stage II or III. <a data-source="sa">HLTH 5485, July 24, 2025, p. 2</a>.</p><div class="grid three severity-grid"><article class="panel"><span class="severity-indicator">I · Localized</span><div id="severity-1"></div><h3>One localized affected area</h3><p>Nodules or abscesses without tunnels or scarring.</p></article><article class="panel"><span class="severity-indicator">II · Recurrent, separated</span><div id="severity-2"></div><h3>Separated areas of involvement</h3><p>Recurrent lesions with tunnels or scarring; sites remain widely separated.</p></article><article class="panel"><span class="severity-indicator">III · Extensive, connected</span><div id="severity-3"></div><h3>Connected extensive involvement</h3><p>Diffuse or near-diffuse involvement with interconnected tunnels and scarring.</p></article></div><p class="caption">Non-clinical, non-graphic schematics, not diagnostic images. <a data-source="review" data-page="37">Review p. 37</a>.</p>
+<section class="card" id="indication"><div class="eyebrow">01 / Define the indication before counting people</div><h2>A condition, a treatment pathway, an eligible subset</h2><div class="grid"><div><p>For this study, we rely on publicly available information and carefully documented clinical and economic assumptions. Our main reference is a pharmacoeconomic review published by <strong>Canada’s Drug Agency / L’Agence des médicaments du Canada (CDA-AMC), formerly the Canadian Agency for Drugs and Technologies in Health (CADTH)</strong>. We focus on <strong>hidradenitis suppurativa (HS)</strong>, a chronic inflammatory skin condition, and <strong>secukinumab</strong>, marketed as <strong>Cosentyx</strong>. <a data-source="review" data-page="9">Review, pp. 9–12, 143–148</a>.</p><p>We illustrate how an economist could construct a BC budget impact analysis; we do not possess actual provincial patient-level records. The reviewed indication is adults with moderate-to-severe HS who responded inadequately to conventional systemic therapy. A diagnosis alone does not establish biologic eligibility or public reimbursement.</p></div><div id="care-art" class="panel"></div></div>
+<h3>Clinical severity: a physician’s assessment</h3><div id="severity-clinician" class="illustration-scroll"></div><p>Hurley stage and inadequate treatment response usually cannot be established from diagnostic codes alone. Clinical documentation refines the estimated cohort. Current BC initial targeted-treatment criteria require Hurley stage II or III. <a data-source="sa">HLTH 5485, July 24, 2025, p. 2</a>.</p><div class="grid three severity-grid"><article class="panel"><span class="severity-indicator">I · Localized</span><div id="severity-1"></div><div class="severity-copy"><h3>One localized affected area</h3><p>Nodules or abscesses without tunnels or scarring.</p></div></article><article class="panel"><span class="severity-indicator">II · Recurrent, separated</span><div id="severity-2"></div><div class="severity-copy"><h3>Separated areas of involvement</h3><p>Recurrent lesions with tunnels or scarring; sites remain widely separated.</p></div></article><article class="panel"><span class="severity-indicator">III · Extensive, connected</span><div id="severity-3"></div><div class="severity-copy"><h3>Connected extensive involvement</h3><p>Diffuse or near-diffuse involvement with interconnected tunnels and scarring.</p></div></article></div><p class="caption">Non-clinical, non-graphic schematics, not diagnostic images. <a data-source="review" data-page="37">Review p. 37</a>.</p>
 <h3>A treatment decision requires a clinical branch</h3><div id="clinical-tree" class="illustration-scroll"></div><p class="caption">A decision algorithm for teaching. Inadequate response prompts an eligibility review; it does not automatically lead to secukinumab. Conventional and adjunctive care may continue as appropriate. <a data-source="review" data-page="37">Review, pp. 37–39</a>.</p></section>
-<section class="card" id="authorization"><div class="eyebrow">Who approves a drug, and who pays for it?</div><h2>From Health Canada’s authorization to BC PharmaCare reimbursement</h2><div class="approval-story" id="approval-story"></div><p class="caption">A simplified pathway: evidence reviews, negotiations and provincial assessment can overlap. Authorization or a positive recommendation does not guarantee reimbursement.</p><div class="grid"><article class="panel"><h3>Research and marketing authorization</h3><p>Manufacturers develop medicines and sponsor clinical research. Health Canada regulates trials conducted in Canada under applicable requirements; it does not conduct every trial. It reviews safety, efficacy and quality evidence for marketing authorization. A Notice of Compliance follows a satisfactory submission review. <a data-source="trials">Clinical-trial guidance</a>; <a data-source="healthcanada">Health Canada NOC explanation</a>.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Evidence, advice and negotiation</h3><p>CDA-AMC assesses comparative clinical and economic evidence, including cost-effectiveness, budget impact, uncertainty, and patient and clinician perspectives. Its reimbursement recommendations advise public plans; they do not automatically require BC coverage. Where applicable, pCPA negotiates pricing arrangements, whose net prices are generally confidential. <a data-source="cdaReviews">CDA-AMC reviews</a>; <a data-source="cdaGuidance">recommendation and next-step guidance, p. 9</a>; <a data-source="pcpa">pCPA process</a>.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Provincial implementation</h3><p>BC makes coverage and implementation decisions through its drug-review and PharmaCare processes. Authorization to sell a medication is different from public reimbursement. Plan eligibility, indication, clinical requirements and pricing policy determine applicable coverage. <a data-source="bcDecisions">BC drug-review decisions</a>; <a data-source="saPolicy">Special Authority policy</a>.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Appraise the assumptions</h3><p>Population size, market shares, uptake, comparator costs, adherence, discontinuation and clinical effects require scrutiny. Sponsor and payer analyses may differ in perspective and interpretation. The historical national secukinumab BIA totals—76,543 versus 9,547 <strong>CAD $000</strong>—illustrate the financial importance of assumptions, without establishing intent or predicting BC expenditure. <a href="#national">Inspect the actual revisions</a>; <a data-source="review" data-page="173">Review, pp. 173–179</a>.</p></article></div></section>
-<section class="card" id="special-authority"><div class="eyebrow">Clinical eligibility → coverage review → plan payment</div><h2>Special Authority: clinical eligibility is not the same as financial coverage</h2><div id="sa-illustration" class="illustration-scroll"></div><p>The current <a data-source="sa">HLTH 5485: Targeted Therapies for Hidradenitis Suppurativa</a> is dated <strong>July 24, 2025</strong> (checked October 20, 2026). Its initial request combines adult age, active moderate-to-severe HS, Hurley II/III, an inadequate response to a recent continuous 90-day optimally dosed oral-antibiotic trial, and specified lesion criteria. A dermatologist documents the findings and body map.</p><div class="panel"><span class="tag hyp">Fictional teaching example · not an official determination</span><div class="toolbar"><label>Request type <select id="sa-mode"><option value="initial">Initial · 6 months</option><option value="switch">Switch · 6 months</option><option value="renewal">Renewal · 1 year</option></select></label></div><div class="controls"><label class="control">Age<input id="sa-age" type="number" min="0" max="110" value="35"></label><label class="control">Current Hurley stage<select id="sa-stage"><option>I</option><option selected>II</option><option>III</option></select></label><label class="control">Abscess + nodule count<input id="sa-an" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="3"></label><label class="control">Distinct anatomical areas<input id="sa-areas" type="number" min="0" max="20" value="2"></label><label class="control">Draining sinus / fistula count<input id="sa-sinus" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="1"></label></div><div class="checks"><label><input id="sa-antibiotic" type="checkbox" checked> Active moderate/severe HS; qualifying antibiotic failure documented</label><label><input id="sa-dermatologist" type="checkbox" checked> Dermatologist assessment and body map documented</label><label><input id="sa-biologic-failure" type="checkbox" checked> Switch example: prior biologic failed to attain/maintain required response</label><label><input id="sa-response" type="checkbox" checked> Renewal example: maintained ≥50% lesion reduction; no increased abscess/fistula count</label></div><p id="sa-result" class="notice" role="status"></p></div><p>Initial requests require ≥3 abscesses/nodules, ≥2 anatomical areas and ≥1 draining sinus/fistula. Switching uses a separate six-month request documenting the prior biologic and response; renewal uses a one-year request documenting maintained response. Only one biologic is covered at a time. <a data-source="sa">Form, pp. 1–2</a>.</p><p class="notice"><strong>Approval ≠ free medication.</strong> Physicians and PharmaCare make actual clinical and coverage decisions under current policies. Approved coverage remains subject to the person’s PharmaCare plan and applicable pricing rules; <a href="#payers">explore payment separately</a>.</p></section>
-<section class="card" id="prevalence-methods"><div class="eyebrow">The first estimation challenge</div><h2>How many residents have the indication?</h2><p>Our first challenge is estimating how many BC residents have the indication. One approach applies a published prevalence rate to a compatible population. Another identifies potential cases directly from linked administrative health records.</p><div class="grid"><article class="panel"><span class="tag">A · Published prevalence estimate</span><div id="prevalence-population-art"></div><div class="formula">N<sub>estimated</sub> = (r / 100,000) × P</div><p class="small">r = prevalence per 100,000; P = population for the same age universe. Both values below are hypothetical, not current BC observations.</p><div class="controls"><label class="control">Age universe<select id="prevalence-scope"><option value="all">All ages · illustrative</option><option value="adult">Adults · illustrative</option></select></label><label class="control">Rate per 100,000<input id="prevalence-rate" type="number" min="0" max="100000" step="10" value="1000"></label><label class="control">Compatible population<input id="prevalence-denominator" type="number" min="0" max="100000000" step="1000" value="5800000"></label></div><div id="prevalence-equation" class="visual-equation"></div><p id="prevalence-explanation"></p></article><article class="panel"><span class="tag blue">B · Administrative case ascertainment</span><div id="prevalence-record-art"></div><p>Merge candidate DAD, MSP and NACRS records, link patient keys, remove duplicate encounters and validate the definition. Physician care can identify a potential case without a hospital admission, so hospital-only counting may undercount people.</p><p>PLIS is supplementary and access-dependent, not a stand-alone HS diagnostic source. Administrative capture depends on diagnosis, care use, coding and the observation window. <a href="#cohort">Explore the linked synthetic cohort</a>.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Condition prevalence ≠ reimbursement eligibility.</strong> Clinical criteria, treatment history, actual utilization and plan eligibility narrow the population further.</div></article></div><div id="prevalence-overlap" class="illustration-scroll"></div><p class="caption">Published prevalence may include undiagnosed people. Administrative ascertainment may miss them or include miscoded cases. Different observation windows and healthcare access also produce differences. The two approaches are not forced to agree; neither replaces the existing <a href="#population">historical adult-cohort model</a>.</p></section>
-<section class="card" id="databases"><div class="eyebrow">02 / Sources contribute different kinds of evidence</div><h2>Four encounter streams, plus medication and payer records</h2><p>Link approved extracts using a project-specific encrypted linkage key. De-identification does not remove the need for governance. These miniature datasets are invented examples, not extracts from provincial systems.</p><div class="grid" id="database-grid"></div><div class="notice">PLIS is supplementary and access is conditional. It never contributes an independent HS-positive set to the cohort union on this page.</div></section>
+<section class="card" id="authorization"><div class="eyebrow">Who approves a drug, and who pays for it?</div><h2>From Health Canada’s authorization to BC PharmaCare reimbursement</h2><div class="approval-story" id="approval-story"></div><p class="caption">A simplified pathway: evidence reviews, negotiations and provincial assessment can overlap. Authorization or a positive recommendation does not guarantee reimbursement.</p><div class="grid"><article class="panel"><h3>Research and marketing authorization</h3><p>Manufacturers develop medicines and sponsor clinical research. Health Canada regulates trials conducted in Canada under applicable requirements; it does not conduct every trial. It reviews safety, efficacy and quality evidence for marketing authorization. A Notice of Compliance (NOC) follows a satisfactory submission review. <a data-source="trials">Clinical-trial guidance</a>; <a data-source="healthcanada">Health Canada Notice of Compliance explanation</a>.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Evidence, advice and negotiation</h3><p>Canada’s Drug Agency (CDA-AMC) assesses comparative clinical and economic evidence, including cost-effectiveness, budget impact, uncertainty, and patient and clinician perspectives. Its reimbursement recommendations advise public plans; they do not automatically require BC coverage. Where applicable, the pan-Canadian Pharmaceutical Alliance (pCPA) negotiates pricing arrangements, whose net prices are generally confidential. <a data-source="cdaReviews">CDA-AMC reviews</a>; <a data-source="cdaGuidance">recommendation and next-step guidance, p. 9</a>; <a data-source="pcpa">pCPA process</a>.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Provincial implementation</h3><p>BC makes coverage and implementation decisions through its drug-review and PharmaCare processes. Authorization to sell a medication is different from public reimbursement. Plan eligibility, indication, clinical requirements and pricing policy determine applicable coverage