+<section class="card" id="special-authority"><div class="eyebrow">Clinical eligibility → coverage review → plan payment</div><h2>Special Authority: clinical eligibility is not the same as financial coverage</h2><p>In British Columbia, Special Authority (SA) is a PharmaCare process for coverage of certain medicines and indications against established criteria. A prescriber generally submits clinical documentation for review; approval allows coverage under the patient’s applicable plan and reimbursement rules and does not necessarily mean the patient pays nothing. <a data-source="saPolicy">Special Authority overview</a>.</p><div id="sa-illustration" class="illustration-scroll"></div><p>The current <a data-source="sa">HLTH 5485: Targeted Therapies for Hidradenitis Suppurativa</a> is dated <strong>July 24, 2025</strong> (checked October 20, 2026). Its initial request combines adult age, active moderate-to-severe HS, Hurley II/III, an inadequate response to a recent continuous 90-day optimally dosed oral-antibiotic trial, and specified lesion criteria. A dermatologist documents the findings and body map.</p><div class="panel"><span class="tag hyp">Fictional teaching example · not an official determination</span><div class="toolbar"><label>Request type <select id="sa-mode"><option value="initial">Initial · 6 months</option><option value="switch">Switch · 6 months</option><option value="renewal">Renewal · 1 year</option></select></label></div><div class="controls"><label class="control">Age<input id="sa-age" type="number" min="0" max="110" value="35"></label><label class="control">Current Hurley stage<select id="sa-stage"><option>I</option><option selected>II</option><option>III</option></select></label><label class="control">Abscess + nodule count<input id="sa-an" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="3"></label><label class="control">Distinct anatomical areas<input id="sa-areas" type="number" min="0" max="20" value="2"></label><label class="control">Draining sinus / fistula count<input id="sa-sinus" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="1"></label></div><div class="checks"><label><input id="sa-antibiotic" type="checkbox" checked> Active moderate/severe HS; qualifying antibiotic failure documented</label><label><input id="sa-dermatologist" type="checkbox" checked> Dermatologist assessment and body map documented</label><label><input id="sa-biologic-failure" type="checkbox" checked> Switch example: prior biologic failed to attain/maintain required response</label><label><input id="sa-response" type="checkbox" checked> Renewal example: maintained ≥50% lesion reduction; no increased abscess/fistula count</label></div><p id="sa-result" class="notice" role="status"></p></div><p>Initial requests require ≥3 abscesses/nodules, ≥2 anatomical areas and ≥1 draining sinus/fistula. Switching uses a separate six-month request documenting the prior biologic and response; renewal uses a one-year request documenting maintained response. Only one biologic is covered at a time. <a data-source="sa">Form, pp. 1–2</a>.</p><p class="notice"><strong>Approval ≠ free medication.</strong> Physicians and PharmaCare make actual clinical and coverage decisions under current policies. Approved coverage remains subject to the person’s PharmaCare plan and applicable pricing rules; <a href="#payers">explore payment separately</a>.</p></section>
+<section class="card" id="prevalence-methods"><div class="eyebrow">The first estimation challenge</div><h2>How many residents have the indication?</h2><p>Our first challenge is estimating how many BC residents have the indication. One approach applies a published prevalence rate to a compatible population. Another identifies potential cases directly from linked administrative health records.</p><div class="grid"><article class="panel"><span class="tag">A · Published prevalence estimate</span><div id="prevalence-population-art"></div><div class="formula">N<sub>estimated</sub> = (r / 100,000) × P</div><p class="small">r = prevalence per 100,000; P = population for the same age universe. Both values below are hypothetical, not current BC observations.</p><div class="controls"><label class="control">Age universe<select id="prevalence-scope"><option value="all">All ages · illustrative</option><option value="adult">Adults · illustrative</option></select></label><label class="control">Rate per 100,000<input id="prevalence-rate" type="number" min="0" max="100000" step="10" value="1000"></label><label class="control">Compatible population<input id="prevalence-denominator" type="number" min="0" max="100000000" step="1000" value="5800000"></label></div><div id="prevalence-equation" class="visual-equation"></div><p id="prevalence-explanation"></p></article><article class="panel"><span class="tag blue">B · Administrative case ascertainment</span><div id="prevalence-record-art"></div><p>Merge candidate Discharge Abstract Database (DAD), Medical Services Plan (MSP) and National Ambulatory Care Reporting System (NACRS) records, link patient keys, remove duplicate encounters and validate the definition. Physician care can identify a potential case without a hospital admission, so hospital-only counting may undercount people.</p><p>Laboratory data can help identify cohorts for indications with validated laboratory-based case definitions. For hidradenitis suppurativa (HS), the Provincial Laboratory Information Solution (PLIS) provides supplementary context, not a stand-alone diagnostic source; access remains conditional. Administrative capture depends on diagnosis, care use, coding and the observation window. <a href="#cohort">Explore the linked synthetic cohort</a>.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Condition prevalence ≠ reimbursement eligibility.</strong> Clinical criteria, treatment history, actual utilization and plan eligibility narrow the population further.</div></article></div><div id="prevalence-overlap" class="illustration-scroll"></div><p class="caption">Published prevalence may include undiagnosed people. Administrative ascertainment may miss them or include miscoded cases. Different observation windows and healthcare access also produce differences. The two approaches are not forced to agree; neither replaces the existing <a href="#population">historical adult-cohort model</a>.</p></section>
