-<section class="card" id="population"><div class="eyebrow">04 / A separate top-down approximation</div><h2>From 4.48 million adults to a small treated cohort</h2><p>Prevalence, diagnosis, severity, biologic eligibility, actual use and public reimbursement describe different populations. This historical model uses <strong>biologic utilization as a proxy</strong> for the treated market; it does not count every clinically eligible untreated adult.</p><div class="formula">N<sub>eligible</sub> = N<sub>adult</sub> × p<sub>HS</sub> × p<sub>diagnosed</sub> × p<sub>moderate/severe</sub> × p<sub>biologic</sub> × p<sub>public</sub></div><div id="population-controls" class="controls"></div><div class="grid"><figure><div id="population-funnel"></div><figcaption>Bars use a common logarithmic count scale to keep small populations visible; labels show actual expected counts. No area encoding is implied.</figcaption></figure><div class="panel"><div id="population-result"></div><button id="use-population">Use top-down count in the BIA</button><p class="small">The historical inputs (including 4,478,159 BC adults) come from the review, pp. 173–178. They are not contemporary BC observations. The approximately 100 publicly funded patients plus 20 past discontinuers on p. 175 are expert historical comparisons, not a 2026 register count.</p><p id="population-comparison"></p></div></div><details><summary>Conditional probabilities, capture and validation</summary><p>Each fraction must refer to the population surviving the preceding stage. Multiplication does not require statistical independence when these are conditional probabilities; multiplying unrelated marginal estimates can be misleading. The report's 3.5% uptake was back-calculated, rather than directly measured from linked BC records.</p><p>A validation-based correction might use N<sub>true</sub> ≈ N<sub>recorded</sub> × PPV / sensitivity, provided the validation sample transports to the study population. This is not an automatic correction for missing data.</p><div class="controls"><label class="control">Assumed sensitivity<input id="sensitivity" type="number" min=".05" max="1" step=".05" value=".8"></label><label class="control">Assumed positive predictive value<input id="ppv" type="number" min="0" max="1" step=".05" value=".9"></label></div><p id="capture-result"></p><p>Capture–recapture is not applied. It would require defensible assumptions about source dependence, a closed population, linkage accuracy and heterogeneous capture probabilities; care-seeking links these databases strongly.</p></details></section>
+<section class="card" id="population"><div class="eyebrow">04 / A separate top-down approximation</div><h2>From 4.48 million adults to a small treated cohort</h2><p>Prevalence, diagnosis, severity, biologic eligibility, actual use and public reimbursement describe different populations. This historical model uses <strong>biologic utilization as a proxy</strong> for the treated market; it does not count every clinically eligible untreated adult.</p><div class="formula">N<sub>eligible</sub> = N<sub>adult</sub> × p<sub>HS</sub> × p<sub>diagnosed</sub> × p<sub>moderate/severe</sub> × p<sub>biologic</sub> × p<sub>public</sub></div><div id="population-controls" class="controls"></div><div class="grid"><figure><div id="population-funnel"></div><figcaption>Bars use a common logarithmic count scale to keep small populations visible; labels show actual expected counts. No area encoding is implied.</figcaption></figure><div class="panel"><div id="population-result"></div><button id="use-population">Use top-down count in the BIA</button><p class="small">The historical inputs (including 4,478,159 BC adults) come from the review, pp. 173–178. They are not contemporary BC observations. The approximately 100 publicly funded patients plus 20 past discontinuers on p. 175 are expert historical comparisons, not a 2026 register count.</p><p id="population-comparison"></p></div></div><details><summary>Conditional probabilities, capture and validation</summary><p>Each fraction must refer to the population surviving the preceding stage. Multiplication does not require statistical independence when these are conditional probabilities; multiplying unrelated marginal estimates can be misleading. The report's 3.5% uptake was back-calculated, rather than directly measured from linked BC records.</p><p>A validation-based correction might use N<sub>true</sub> ≈ N<sub>recorded</sub> × positive predictive value (PPV) / sensitivity, provided the validation sample transports to the study population.