 <section class="card" id="market"><div class="eyebrow">05 / Define the substitution market</div><h2>Adalimumab biosimilars are the principal displaced biologic</h2><div class="grid"><div><p>The reference scenario continues adalimumab biosimilars in a defined eligible biologic cohort. New initiators enter that same market. Secukinumab replaces some biologic exposure in the counterfactual new-drug scenario.</p><p>Systemic antibiotics, wound care, topical treatment and procedures have different roles and may be concomitant. Antibiotic use alone never makes someone eligible to switch. Other historical off-label biologic options discussed in the review are not assigned unsupported market shares here. <a data-source="review" data-page="37">Review, pp. 37–39, 173</a>.</p><div class="pathway"><div>Conventional / adjunct therapy<br><small>Outside the modelled biologic market</small></div><div>Biologic-eligible but untreated<br><small>Not automatically a switcher</small></div><div>Active adalimumab biosimilar use<br><small>Reference market = 100%</small></div></div></div><div class="panel"><label>Flow year <select id="flow-year"><option value="0">Year 1</option><option value="1">Year 2</option><option value="2">Year 3</option></select></label><div id="market-flow"></div><p class="caption">Live stock-flow diagram. Flow widths are schematic; labels and the transition table reconcile exactly. New initiations and exits are visible when enabled.</p></div></div><h3>Patient cohort and treatment transitions</h3><p class="small">Estimated patient counts, Years 1–3</p><div class="scroll transition-table" id="flow-table"></div><p class="caption">Displayed patient counts are rounded; calculations use unrounded expected values. Patient-years are exposure, not discrete people, and retain one decimal. Independently rounded rows may differ by one.</p></section>
 <section class="card" id="pricing"><div class="eyebrow">06 / Acquisition, induction and maintenance</div><h2>A dose schedule is an expenditure schedule</h2><p>Historical source benchmarks: <strong>$882.59 per 150 mg secukinumab syringe/pen</strong> and <strong>$471.27 per 40 mg adalimumab biosimilar syringe/pen</strong>. Two 150 mg units make a 300 mg dose ($1,765.18 before adjustments). These are not current confidential BC net prices. Table 8 uses sponsor-submitted secukinumab prices and comparator formulary prices accessed June–July 2023. <a data-source="review" data-page="165">Review, pp. 146, 165–167</a>.</p><div id="dose-math" class="panel"><div class="toolbar"><label>Cost illustration <select id="dose-math-period"><option value="first">First treatment year</option><option value="maintenance">Maintenance year</option></select></label></div><div id="dose-equation" class="illustration-scroll"></div><p id="dose-math-detail"></p><div id="exposure-equation" class="notice"></div></div><div id="price-controls" class="controls"></div><div class="controls"><label class="control">Secukinumab base maintenance interval<select id="frequency"><option value="4">Every 4 weeks (reviewed final monograph)</option><option value="2">Every 2 weeks (escalated regimen)</option></select><small>Loading at weeks 0, 1, 2, 3, 4; maintenance begins at week 8 or 6.</small></label></div><div class="notice"><strong>Do not transplant the report's every-two-week costs into monthly dosing.</strong> The original economic submission used the more frequent regimen. This tool independently counts administration events. Its exact 52-week totals differ from the report's daily/annual conventions. Review pp. 9–12, 146, 165 and 174.</div>
 <div class="toolbar"><label>52-week dose calendar <select id="calendar-drug"><option value="S4">Secukinumab · every 4 weeks</option><option value="S2">Secukinumab · every 2 weeks</option><option value="A">Adalimumab · weekly maintenance</option></select></label><label>Treatment year <select id="calendar-year"><option value="0">First year</option><option value="1">Maintenance year</option></select></label></div><div class="week-grid" id="dose-calendar"></div><p class="caption">Weeks 0–51 for initiation; weeks 52–103 for maintenance. Orange = initiation phase; green = maintenance; each cell is keyboard focusable. Doses at the right boundary belong to the next year. Adalimumab: 160 mg week 0, 80 mg week 2, then 40 mg weekly from week 4. <a data-source="review" data-page="39">Review p. 39</a>.</p><div class="grid four" id="cost-cards"></div><figure><div id="cost-bars"></div><figcaption>Initiation acquisition (orange) + maintenance acquisition (green) + optional fees/markup/administration (blue). Calendar is a regimen viewer; the base-interval and escalation controls determine model costs.</figcaption></figure><details><summary>Unit-cost arithmetic and exact annual calculation</summary><div class="scroll" id="cost-table"></div><p>Cost = Σ events [units × unit price × (1 − discount) × (1 + acquisition growth)<sup>forecast year−1</sup> × (1 + markup) + dispensing fee + administration cost]. Fees are per administration as an explicit simplification. When the base interval is q2w, everyone already receives that regimen and the escalation fraction has no additional effect. The escalation fraction mixes complete q4w and q2w courses; it is not an estimated clinical escalation rate.</p><p>For established patients, continue the original event schedule across annual boundaries. Patients switching later in a year incur their prior adalimumab events only before the switch and secukinumab events thereafter. No dose is charged twice at a boundary. Dosing units can be fractional only when averaging expected patients or regimen mixtures.</p></details></section>
-<section class="card" id="switching"><div class="eyebrow">07 / The central policy experiment</div><h2>Patient stocks are different from new switches</h2><span class="tag hyp">Requested teaching scenario · 30% / 40% / 50%</span><p>Cumulative shares refer to the active eligible biologic cohort receiving secukinumab at the end of each model year. They are not probabilities applied successively to everyone remaining on adalimumab.</p><div class="toolbar"><label>Scenario preset <select id="preset"><option value="main">Main illustrative · 30 / 40 / 50%</option><option value="conservative">Conservative uptake · 5 / 10 / 15%</option><option value="high">High uptake · 50 / 65 / 80%</option><option value="historical">CDA-AMC historical shares · 10 / 20 / 30%</option><option value="fixed">Fixed 120-patient demonstration · no growth</option></select></label><button id="reset">Reset all assumptions</button><button id="export">Export live model + assumptions CSV</button></div><div id="switch-controls" class="controls"></div><div class="formula">Fixed 120 patients: stocks = (36, 48, 60); newly switched = (36, 12, 12).<br>With no exits: new switches<sub>t</sub> = N<sub>t</sub>q<sub>t</sub> − N<sub>t−1</sub>q<sub>t−1</sub>.</div><div id="switch-summary"></div><figure><div id="stock-chart"></div><figcaption>Bars show end-of-year stocks; dots show annual gross new switches. Fractional values represent expected counts, not partial people in the record explorer.</figcaption></figure><details><summary>Advanced transitions, within-year switching and public cost weight</summary><div id="advanced-controls" class="controls"></div><p>Year 1 begins with the entered active cohort. From Year 2, shared mortality, migration/loss and discontinuation act sequentially on both scenarios; growth adds previous active stock × growth, plus separately entered initiations. New users begin on adalimumab before target-share allocation. Returning secukinumab users restart adalimumab with loading; remaining adalimumab users supply replacement switches to maintain target shares. Returners are protected from immediately switching again. If replacement capacity is insufficient, the achieved share is lower and reported in the table.</p><div class="formula">N<sub>t</sub> = N<sub>t−1</sub>(1−m)(1−l)(1−d) + gN<sub>t−1</sub> + I<sub>t</sub></div><p>Exits occur at year boundaries, not continuously; the model excludes costs after exit and does not estimate post-discontinuation background care. Growth is growth in use, not incidence or BC population growth. The 10% default is a historical CDA-AMC assumption, not an observed current BC trend.</p></details></section>
