 <section class="card" id="payers"><div class="eyebrow">09 / Patient expenditure is a separate question</div><h2>Who ultimately pays the pharmacy bill?</h2><div class="grid"><div><p>Fair PharmaCare uses family-income-related deductibles and maxima. Under regular assistance, eligible costs are paid by the family until the deductible; PharmaCare then pays 70% until the family maximum and 100% thereafter. Enhanced assistance generally pays 75% after the deductible for qualifying families; plan rules and eligible costs still apply. Special Authority approval does not eliminate plan rules. <a data-source="fair">BC assistance levels, January 2026</a>.</p><p>The illustration below assumes every modelled charge is eligible, no other family claims, annual reset, and private insurance pays an entered proportion of the residual <em>after</em> public adjudication. It is not a benefit-adjudication calculator and does not feed the aggregate BIA.</p><div class="toolbar"><label>Patient payer example <select id="payer-plan"><option value="fair">Fair PharmaCare</option><option value="full">Eligible full-coverage plan</option><option value="other">Hypothetical non-public coverage</option></select></label><label>Fair assistance <select id="assistance-rate"><option value="regular">Regular · 70% public</option><option value="enhanced">Enhanced · 75% public</option></select></label></div><div class="controls"><label class="control">Annual family deductible (CAD)<input id="deductible" type="number" min="0" max="100000" step="50" value="650"></label><label class="control">Annual family maximum (CAD)<input id="maximum" type="number" min="0" max="100000" step="50" value="900"></label><label class="control">Private share of residual (%)<input id="private-rate" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="0"><output id="private-label">0%</output></label></div><button id="income-example">Load official $30,589 income example: $650 / $900</button><p class="small">Illustrative income example from BC's <a href="https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/health/health-drug-coverage/pharmacare-for-bc-residents/who-we-cover/fair-pharmacare-plan">Fair PharmaCare explanation</a>. Use the official assistance table for other income bands.</p></div><div class="panel"><div id="pharmacy-art"></div><div id="payer-donut"></div></div></div><h3>How Fair PharmaCare eligible family spending accumulates</h3><div id="fair-progress" class="fair-progress"></div><p id="coverage-note" class="caption"></p><p>Plan C (income assistance and family services) and Plan W (First Nations Health Benefits, funded by FNHA) can cover 100% of eligible products for enrolled eligible people. Their formularies and coverage rules still apply, including Special Authority where required. This is not a statement that every medicine is covered; Plan G or Plan NP coverage is not assumed for secukinumab HS. <a data-source="planC">Plan C</a>; <a data-source="planW">Plan W</a>; <a data-source="bcPlans">BC PharmaCare plans</a>.</p><div id="payer-bars"></div><div class="scroll" id="payer-table"></div><p id="payer-reconcile" class="live-status"></p><details><summary>Recorded non-public amounts are not necessarily out-of-pocket burden</summary><p>PharmaNet is the dispensing and claims network; PharmaCare is a public benefit program. A research extract may include total charges and PharmaCare-paid amounts, depending on the authorized data dictionary. Do not assume the difference was personally paid: direct billing, later private reimbursement, federal plans and other payments may not be fully visible. Confirm available fields and coordination-of-benefits definitions. Patient-facing PharmaNet medication records do not include dispense costs. <a data-source="pharma">BC About PharmaNet, June 2026</a>.</p><p>Transportation, wound-care supplies, lost work and other non-drug costs are excluded here. Adding them requires separate quantities, sources and a wider perspective; they cannot silently enter an acquisition-only public BIA.</p></details></section>
-<section class="card" id="uncertainty"><div class="eyebrow">10 / Uncertainty is part of the result</div><h2>Which assumptions change the decision's financial scale?</h2><div class="toolbar"><label>One-way range ± <input id="tornado-range" type="number" min="1" max="90" step="5" value="25" style="width:80px"> %</label><span class="small">Multiplicative ranges; probabilities bounded to [0,1].</span></div><figure><div id="tornado"></div><figcaption>Each row changes one factor while holding others fixed. Diagnosis and biologic uptake rescale the BIA cohort, representing a top-down interpretation. Their bars are alternatives to changing N, not independent additions. Frequency compares q4w with q2w. A zero discontinuation baseline uses 0 to the selected absolute range.</figcaption></figure><p id="tornado-note"></p><details open><summary>Reproducible Monte Carlo demonstration</summary><p>These distributions are assumed for teaching; they are not fitted to BC records. For a proportion with mean μ and concentration κ, p ~ Beta(μκ, (1−μ)κ). Means at 0 or 1 remain fixed. Positive costs use lognormal factors with σ² = log(1 + CV²), log-mean −σ²/2; cohort size uses a mean-one lognormal factor with log-SD 0.2. Independently drawn share values are sorted to preserve increasing uptake, so their marginal distributions are changed by ordering. Other inputs remain fixed.</p><div class="controls"><label class="control">Simulation draws<select id="draws"><option>250</option><option selected>500</option><option>1000</option></select></label><label class="control">Random seed<input id="seed" type="number" min="1" max="4294967295" value="20261019"></label><label class="control">Beta concentration κ<input id="concentration" type="number" min="5" max="1000" value="60"></label><label class="control">Cost coefficient of variation<input id="cv" type="number" min=".01" max="1" step=".01" value=".15"></label></div><button id="run-mc">Run / repeat simulation</button><p id="mc-status" class="live-status" role="status">Run the simulation for the current assumptions.</p><div class="grid four" id="mc-metrics"></div><figure><div id="histogram"></div><figcaption>Three-year public budget impact distribution, CAD $000. Percentiles describe the entered illustrative model, not a confidence interval for a real BC forecast.</figcaption></figure><p id="mc-check" class="small"></p></details></section>
-<section class="card" id="national"><div class="eyebrow">11 / Interpret the historical review</div><h2>The same drug, very different national forecasts</h2><p class="unit-banner">National historical budget impact · <strong>CAD $000</strong> · rounded</p><p>Canada's Drug Agency (<strong>CDA-AMC</strong>), formerly CADTH, reported the following three-year incremental impacts across participating Canadian public plans. These are <strong>national historical results</strong>, separate from the BC calculator.</p><div class="grid"><div class="panel"><span class="tag hyp">Sponsor-submitted</span><div class="national">76,543</div><div class="national-bar"></div><p class="small">Higher biologic uptake and switching assumptions</p></div><div class="panel"><span class="tag">Combined CDA-AMC reanalysis</span><div class="national">9,547</div><div class="national-bar" style="width:12.473%;background:var(--spring)"></div><p class="small">About 87.5% lower; a change in assumptions, not a measured BC saving</p></div></div><p><a data-source="review" data-page="173">Review Appendix 5, pp. 173–179, Tables 18–20</a>. No BC allocation is obtained by multiplying these totals by population share.</p>
