+<section class="card" id="national"><div class="eyebrow">11 / Interpret the historical review</div><h2>Two historical estimates for participating Canadian public drug plans</h2><p class="unit-banner">Three-year incremental budget impact · <strong>CAD millions</strong> · historical model estimates</p><p>Canada’s Drug Agency / L’Agence des médicaments du Canada (<strong>CDA-AMC</strong>), formerly CADTH, compared the sponsor-submitted budget impact analysis (BIA) with its own reanalysis for participating Canadian public drug plans, including British Columbia. These are <strong>alternative historical model estimates</strong>, not a Canada-versus-BC comparison or observed expenditure following reimbursement.</p><div class="grid"><div class="panel"><span class="tag hyp">Sponsor-submitted BIA</span><div class="national">76.54</div><div class="national-bar"></div><p class="small">Higher biologic uptake and switching assumptions</p></div><div class="panel"><span class="tag">CDA-AMC reanalysis</span><div class="national">9.55</div><div class="national-bar" style="width:12.473%;background:var(--spring)"></div><p class="small">Revised assumptions and corrections; not a unit-price discount or observed BC saving</p></div></div><p><a data-source="review" data-page="173">Review Appendix 5, pp. 173–179, Tables 18–20</a>. Revisions to eligible patients, biologic utilization, public coverage, growth and switching materially changed the forecast. The totals cannot be allocated to BC mechanically by population share, and their ratio is not a difference in unit medication price.</p>
+<p>A national analysis can inform BC’s assessment, but the aggregate is not necessarily representative of its payer population or financial arrangements. A BC analysis needs local population and actual eligible patients, existing treatment use and market shares, public-plan eligibility, negotiated medication prices, local reimbursement policies and patient cost-sharing arrangements. The historical analysis used provincial inputs, including BC; it does not establish BC’s realised expenditure or fully describe confidential net-price arrangements.</p><p class="small"><strong>Cost per treated patient:</strong> Comparisons across jurisdictions require a comparable expenditure numerator, treated-population denominator, treatment exposure, pricing basis and payer perspective. Dividing these two budget-impact totals by arbitrary patient counts would be misleading because their population and utilization assumptions differ; no per-patient amounts are inferred here.</p><div class="pathway"><div><strong>Biologic utilization</strong><br>14% → 3.5%<br><small>Smaller treatment market</small></div><b>→</b><div><strong>BC coverage factor</strong><br>41% → 73%<br><small>More public eligibility</small></div><b>→</b><div><strong>Biologic-use growth</strong><br>2.5% → 10%<br><small>Table 18 assumption revision</small></div><b>→</b><div><strong>Existing-user switching</strong><br>54/59/61% → 10/20/30%<br><small>Lower displacement</small></div></div><p class="small">Table 17 separately labels 2.85% yearly population growth. Table 18 describes the revised biologic-growth comparison as 2.5% versus 10%; these are reported as labelled, not silently harmonized.</p>
+<details open><summary>Separate revision analyses are not additive contributions</summary><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Reported analysis, Table 19</th><th class="num">Three-year budget impact (CAD millions)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Submitted base case</td><td class="num">76.54</td></tr><tr><td>Correction: remove fees, markups and copayments</td><td class="num">73.20</td></tr><tr><td>Population revision alone, after correction</td><td class="num">18.30</td></tr><tr><td>Public-coverage revision alone, after correction</td><td class="num">80.77</td></tr><tr><td>Biologic-growth revision alone, after correction</td><td class="num">87.01</td></tr><tr><td>Switching revision alone, after correction</td><td class="num">27.06</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Correction + all four revisions</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>9.55</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These separate reanalyses each start after correction; their differences cannot be summed into a national waterfall. Interactions between population, coverage, growth and switching matter. The review could not fully resolve discontinuation, adalimumab dose escalation or subsequent switching because of limitations in the submitted model (pp. 176–177). This page does not claim to reproduce its underlying workbook.</p></details></section>
+<section class="card" id="methods"><div class="eyebrow">12 / Methodology and practical implementation</div><h2>Build an auditable cohort and an auditable counterfactual</h2><div class="grid"><article class="panel"><h3>Case ascertainment</h3><p>Specify index date, lookback, residency, adult age and a common observation window. Define point prevalence at a date or period prevalence over an interval; incident cases require an adequate prior washout. Verify coding versions, diagnostic positions and encounter completeness before applying candidate rules.</p><p>Repeated encounters measure care use, not new people. Link across systems, deduplicate encounters, then count unique patients. Missing diagnoses, delayed diagnosis and misclassification can bias both numerator and eligibility. NACRS non-capture does not prove no emergency care. Codes rarely establish Hurley stage or inadequate response. Validated laboratory-based definitions may identify cohorts for other indications; for HS, PLIS remains supplementary and access-dependent.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Population estimation</h3><p>Top-down approximations and bottom-up records estimate different constructs. Access to care, diagnosis capture, enrollment, indication confirmation and private treatment can all explain disagreement. Validate against sampled charts or registries, report sensitivity and positive predictive value (PPV) with transportability limitations, and retain alternative definitions.</p><p>Growth in biologic utilization is distinct from population growth or incidence. Do not apply multiple growth factors to the same incoming patients. Keep actively treated patients distinct from historical discontinuers and untreated eligible patients.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Economic interpretation</h3><p>The public perspective includes public costs; patient burden and private payments need separate information. List prices differ from confidential net acquisition costs. State price date, unit, pack size, fees, markups and treatment duration. No rebates are inferred here.</p><p>Induction belongs to new treatment courses, not every established patient every year. Include nonadherence, escalation and discontinuation in transparent scenarios. Annual impact is a flow; cumulative impact is the sum. Round only displayed outputs and identify expected counts versus discrete synthetic people.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Affordability versus value</h3><p>A BIA asks how much a program may spend over a specified horizon under alternative treatment markets. Cost-effectiveness analysis also considers health outcomes relative to costs, often using quality-adjusted life-years (QALYs) and a longer horizon. A favourable BIA does not establish value or comparative effectiveness.</p><p>This counterfactual uses only adalimumab biosimilars as the reference biologic. It excludes broader current-market changes, clinical outcomes, adverse-event costs, background treatment, wound care and service offsets. It is a teaching model, not a decision-ready submission.</p></article></div>
+<details class="terminology" id="terminology"><summary>Abbreviations and terminology</summary><dl><dt>BC; CAD</dt><dd>British Columbia; Canadian dollars. CAD $000 means thousands of Canadian dollars.</dd><dt>BIA; BI</dt><dd>Budget impact analysis; budget impact (incremental expenditure).</dd><dt>CDA-AMC; CADTH</dt><dd>Canada’s Drug Agency / L’Agence des médicaments du Canada; formerly the Canadian Agency for Drugs and Technologies in Health.</dd><dt>pCPA</dt><dd>pan-Canadian Pharmaceutical Alliance.</dd><dt>DAD</dt><dd>Discharge Abstract Database.</dd><dt>MSP</dt><dd>Medical Services Plan.</dd><dt>NACRS</dt><dd>National Ambulatory Care Reporting System.</dd><dt>PLIS</dt><dd>Provincial Laboratory Information Solution; laboratory information is supplementary in this HS example, with access conditional.</dd><dt>HS; SA</dt><dd>Hidradenitis suppurativa; Special Authority.</dd><dt>ICD variants</dt><dd>International Classification of Diseases: ICD-9 is the ninth revision; ICD-9-CM is its Clinical Modification; ICD-10 is the tenth revision; ICD-10-CA is the Canadian enhancement. These systems and the detail stored in billing fields are not interchangeable.</dd><dt>DIN; SQL</dt><dd>Drug Identification Number; Structured Query Language.</dd><dt>CIHI; CMDB</dt><dd>Canadian Institute for Health Information; Canadian Management Information System Database.</dd><dt>PPV; QALYs</dt><dd>Positive predictive value; quality-adjusted life-years.</dd><dt>CV; q4w; q2w</dt><dd>Coefficient of variation; every four weeks; every two weeks.</dd><dt>CRP; ALT; units</dt><dd>C-reactive protein; alanine aminotransferase. mg/L means milligrams per litre; U/L means units per litre. The laboratory values shown are synthetic, not HS diagnostic thresholds.</dd><dt>S; A; N</dt><dd>In compact model tables, S denotes secukinumab, A denotes adalimumab and N denotes a patient-population count. Treatment exposure is reported in patient-years.</dd><dt>ISPOR; NOC</dt><dd>International Society for Pharmacoeconomics and Outcomes Research, also known as ISPOR—The Professional Society for Health Economics and Outcomes Research; Notice of Compliance.</dd><dt>AI; SVG</dt><dd>Artificial intelligence; Scalable Vector Graphics. These terms describe the original artwork and browser diagrams.</dd><dt>HLTH 5485; identifiers</dt><dd>The BC health-form identifier for the targeted-therapies request. PAT-001 and other patient keys, and DEMO drug identifiers, are fictional. CSV means comma-separated values; PDF means Portable Document Format.</dd></dl></details>
 <details><summary>Privacy, permissions and an operational research protocol</summary><ol><li>Confirm an approved research question, data custodians, population, observation years and appropriate ethics/governance arrangements.</li><li>Request authorized DAD, MSP, NACRS and medication/financial extracts with versioned data dictionaries. PLIS availability must be independently confirmed.</li><li>Use project-specific linkage keys in an approved environment; assess linkage errors and suppress disclosive aggregates.</li><li>Validate candidate case definitions and clinical eligibility proxies; document missingness, code truncation and regional reporting coverage.</li><li>Reconcile annual treatment stocks and flows against dispensations; confirm indication and payer definitions.</li><li>Version prices, schedules, scenario assumptions and numerical tests. Release only simulated or genuinely public aggregates.</li></ol><p>No identifiable records belong on this public page. Research access to approved extracts is different from clinical access to the live PharmaNet system. No BC patient-level data were supplied or accessed.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Source-labelled assumptions and calculation downloads</summary><p>Editable values below describe defaults; the CSV export also records current settings. Evidence categories distinguish source benchmarks from hypothetical controls.</p><div class="scroll" id="assumptions-table"></div><p><a href="js/model.js">Pure calculation engine</a> · <a href="js/assumptions.js">Structured evidence and assumptions</a> · <a href="scripts/test-model.mjs">Automated numerical tests</a> · <a href="README.md">Implementation and limitations</a></p></details></section>
-<section class="card" id="limitations"><div class="eyebrow">Interpret the forecast conditionally</div><h2>Limitations and Sources of Uncertainty</h2><div class="limitations-grid"><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-0"></div><h3>Prevalence uncertainty</h3><p>Published estimates may not transfer to BC because age structure, care use and diagnosis capture differ. A different denominator or rate changes the eligible-cohort scale.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-1"></div><h3>Administrative identification</h3><p>False positives, false negatives, repeated encounters and missing records affect case counts. Deduplication removes repeated people but does not eliminate diagnostic misclassification.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-2"></div><h3>Clinical eligibility</h3><p>Severity and inadequate prior response are rarely fully observable in billing or discharge codes. Chart review, registries or validated proxies may materially change the eligible subset.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-3"></div><h3>Treatment switching</h3><p>The 30%, 40% and 50% default cumulative shares are hypothetical. Different uptake changes treatment stocks, displaced comparator use and the forecast.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-4"></div><h3>Drug prices</h3><p>Public benchmark prices may differ from confidential government net prices. Biosimilar prices, negotiated arrangements, fees and markups can change the incremental impact.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-5"></div><h3>Dosing and adherence</h3><p>Escalation, delayed injections, missed doses and discontinuation change realized use. Recommended schedules alone do not establish actual acquisition expenditure.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-6"></div><h3>Clinical effectiveness</h3><p>Comparative efficacy and tolerability remain uncertain. Response and discontinuation affect utilization; no secukinumab superiority over adalimumab is assumed.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-7"></div><h3>Public coverage and patient burden</h3><p>Plan eligibility, Special Authority, deductibles, private coverage and pricing rules affect payment. A public BIA does not represent the patient’s complete financial burden.</p></article></div><h3>Change the assumptions, change the budget</h3><p>These model-derived scenarios vary the current cohort and switching shares together. They are illustrative contrasts, not confidence intervals or a ranking guaranteed under every price configuration.</p><div id="limitations-scenarios" class="grid three"></div><p class="notice">The estimated budget impact is conditional on the patient population, treatment behaviour, medication prices, and reimbursement rules assumed in the analysis.</p><p class="caption">Clinical and economic uncertainty: <a data-source="review" data-page="147">review, pp. 147–148, 175–179</a>. Administrative and payer limitations are also documented alongside their source-specific modules.</p></section>
+<section class="card" id="limitations"><div class="eyebrow">Interpret the forecast conditionally</div><h2>Limitations and Sources of Uncertainty</h2><div class="limitations-grid"><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-0"></div><h3>Prevalence uncertainty</h3><p>Published estimates may not transfer to BC because age structure, care use and diagnosis capture differ. A different denominator or rate changes the eligible-cohort scale.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-1"></div><h3>Administrative identification</h3><p>False positives, false negatives, repeated encounters and missing records affect case counts. Deduplication removes repeated people but does not eliminate diagnostic misclassification. Laboratory-based case definitions can support other indications; PLIS does not independently diagnose HS and its availability must be confirmed.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-2"></div><h3>Clinical eligibility</h3><p>Severity and inadequate prior response are rarely fully observable in billing or discharge codes. Chart review, registries or validated proxies may materially change the eligible subset.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-3"></div><h3>Treatment switching</h3><p>The 30%, 40% and 50% default cumulative shares are hypothetical. Different uptake changes treatment stocks, displaced comparator use and the forecast.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-4"></div><h3>Drug prices</h3><p>Public benchmark prices may differ from confidential government net prices. Biosimilar prices, negotiated arrangements, fees and markups can change the incremental impact.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-5"></div><h3>Dosing and adherence</h3><p>Escalation, delayed injections, missed doses and discontinuation change realized use. Recommended schedules alone do not establish actual acquisition expenditure.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-6"></div><h3>Clinical effectiveness</h3><p>Comparative efficacy and tolerability remain uncertain. Response and discontinuation affect utilization; no secukinumab superiority over adalimumab is assumed.</p></article><article class="limitation-card"><div id="limitation-icon-7"></div><h3>Public coverage and patient burden</h3><p>Plan eligibility, Special Authority, deductibles, private coverage and pricing rules affect payment. A public BIA does not represent the patient’s complete financial burden.</p></article></div><h3>Change the assumptions, change the budget</h3><p>These model-derived scenarios vary the current cohort and switching shares together. They are illustrative contrasts, not confidence intervals or a ranking guaranteed under every price configuration.</p><div id="limitations-scenarios" class="grid three"></div><p class="notice">The estimated budget impact is conditional on the patient population, treatment behaviour, medication prices, and reimbursement rules assumed in the analysis.</p><p class="caption">Clinical and economic uncertainty: <a data-source="review" data-page="147">review, pp. 147–148, 175–179</a>. Administrative and payer limitations are also documented alongside their source-specific modules.</p></section>
